**Context:** HyperDebug's firmware allows commands [up to 256 characters](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/board/hyperdebug/board.h;l=113-118?q=board%2Fhyperdebug%2Fboard.h&ss=chromiumos%2Fchromiumos%2Fcodesearch), but the EC firmware is using a USB stream <-> USART host console interface with an RX buffer defined as being [64 bytes](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/common/usb_console_stream.c;l=29) ([max USB packet size](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/include/usb_descriptor.h;l=17?q=include%2Fusb_descriptor.h&ss=chromiumos%2Fchromiumos%2Fcodesearch)). The EC firmware itself just reads a character at a time from this buffer, and so when we send a command that is too long, the receive buffer is not emptied in time and some characters written to the host console are silently dropped. I encountered this issue when looking at implementing quad-SPI bitbanging on Hyperdebug, where the command length exceeds 64 bytes when enough pin names are provided for bitbanging, but this can be easily reproduced with a patch like the following to pad Hyperdebug commands (where we then see HyperDebug time out, as the command is not echoed as expected):

```patch
index 3ada723267..9b1c8b910a 100644
--- a/sw/host/opentitanlib/src/transport/hyperdebug/mod.rs
+++ b/sw/host/opentitanlib/src/transport/hyperdebug/mod.rs
@@ -569,6 +569,9 @@ impl Conn {
     fn execute_command(&self, cmd: &str, mut callback: impl FnMut(&str)) -> Result<()> {
         let port: &mut TTYPort = &mut self.console_port.borrow_mut();
 
+        let padding = String::from_utf8(vec![b' '; 80])?;
+        let cmd = format!("{}{}{}", &padding, cmd, &padding);
+
         if self.first_use.get() {
             // Send Ctrl-C, followed by the command, then newline.  This will discard any previous
             // partial input, before executing our command.
```

This PR hence introduces a mechanism for chunking Hyperdebug commands that are > 58 characters (leaving a few bytes leeway, see comment in code) to avoid overflowing the buffer. I've tried to change the existing behaviour as *little* as possible whilst introducing the additional chunking logic, and also tried to separate out the response parsing logic into sequential isolated components to make it easier to follow. However, to ease the implementation, I have separated out the Ctrl-C transmission used for the first command sent to clear existing output. This should have negligible performance impact, as it should only happen once on the first command. Otherwise, all commands < 58 bytes will transmit as before, whilst longer commands will be chunked into multiple transmissions.

To test this change, either try a bitbanging command with 7 pins, or use the patch provided above.

*Aside*: I'm relatively new to Rust, so please do point out any improvements you spot.

