This adds `InstantAuthContext` and `useCurrentUser` to the react and react-native packages

## Problem

`db.useAuth` requires type assertions even when we already know for sure we are authed

```tsx
// Imagine we already authed earlier
function MyComponent() {
  const { user } = db.useAuth();
  // Need user! type assertions since TypeScript doesn't know user is guaranteed
  return <div>Hello {user!.email}</div>;
}
```

## Solution

Added `InstantAuthContext` and `useCurrentUser` hook to eliminate loading states and null checks in child components:

```tsx
iimport { init, InstantAuthContext, useCurrentUser } from '@instantdb/react';
import { schema } from '../instant.schema.ts';
                                                                             
const db = init({ appId: 'your-app-id', schema });
                                                                             
function App() {
  const { user, isLoading, error } = db.useAuth();
  if (isLoading) return null; // or a loading spinner
  if (error) return <div>Error: {error.message}</div>;
                                                                             
                                                                             
  return (
    <InstantAuthContext.Provider value={user}>
      <MyComponent />
    </InstantAuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
                                                                             
function MyComponent() {
  const user = useLoggedInUser(); // Never null when user is authenticated
  return <div>Hello {user.email}</div>;
}

```

## Next Steps

To make this pattern more ergonomic we can add an `InstantAuthProvider` component with some defaults for loading, error, and login states. We can also allow users to supply their own logic for those. 

Wanted to put this up though to get thoughts. If we like this can go ahead and add `InstantAuthProvider` and then some docs.

